// Expected: a0 at one session, a1 at two.
//
// The oracle attack of session_nonce_cross.vp, carried out through SIGNVERIF
// instead of ASSERT. Bob signs the concatenation of the challenge he received
// and a nonce of his own, and Alice verifies it. With one Bob the only
// signature that satisfies her check is the one he honestly produced for her,
// and reproducing it is a replay rather than a forgery.
//
// With two Bobs the attacker has a signing oracle that is not itself
// dishonest: it feeds session 1's challenge to session 2's Bob, collects a
// signature over CONCAT(na, nb#2), and presents it to session 1's Alice
// together with nb#2. Her SIGNVERIF passes on a signature no Bob ever made for
// her, over a nonce she never saw chosen.
//
// The two code paths matter. ASSERT compares two terms the recipient built,
// while SIGNVERIF runs a rewrite rule with its own matching and filter, and
// the session-sibling carve-out has to reach the second one too — declining
// this as a replay would lose a genuine attack.

attacker[active]

principal Bob[
	knows private sk
	generates nb
	pk = PUBKEY(sk)
]

Bob -> Alice: [pk]

principal Alice[
	generates na
]

Alice -> Bob: na

principal Bob[
	sig = SIGN(sk, CONCAT(na, nb))
]

Bob -> Alice: sig, nb

principal Alice[
	_ = SIGNVERIF(pk, CONCAT(na, nb), sig)?
	_ = HASH(sig)
]

queries[
	authentication? Bob -> Alice: sig
]
